@page "/docs/cli/account"

@* izzy login, logout and account, and IZZY_KEY (src/IzzyBlazor.Cli/AccountSession.cs; the account service is
   the private izzyblazor-account repo). What the CLI sends is a promise the Privacy page repeats: keep the
   three in step. *@

<ArticlePage Section="cli" Slug="account">
    <Lead>
        The app starters, the blocks and the kits come with a free IzzyBlazor account. <Code>izzy login</Code> signs
        your machine in. After that, izzy creates them on your machine, online or offline.
    </Lead>
    <ChildContent>
        <Heading Level="2">Sign in</Heading>
        <Code Block Language="bash">izzy login</Code>
        <TextBlock>
            izzy shows a code and opens <Anchor Href="https://account.izzyblazor.com">account.izzyblazor.com</Anchor> in
            your browser. Sign in there with an email link, or create your account the same way. Check that the page
            shows the same code as your terminal, then approve it.
        </TextBlock>
        <TextBlock>
            On a machine without a browser, such as a server you reach over SSH, add <Code>--no-browser</Code>. Open
            the address izzy prints on any other device and approve the code there.
        </TextBlock>
        <TextBlock>
            izzy then downloads the account's templates. To list them, run <Code>izzy list</Code>. To create an app
            from one, see <Anchor Href="docs/cli/templates">Templates</Anchor>.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">Work offline</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            izzy keeps your sign-in and the templates in the <Code>.izzy</Code> folder of your home folder. It creates
            apps and blocks from that copy, so you don't need a connection.
        </TextBlock>
        <TextBlock>
            The sign-in renews by itself when you're online. After you update izzy, it downloads the templates for the
            new version the next time you create something.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">What izzy sends</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            izzy creates your code on your machine. It never sends your code, your app's name or which templates you
            used. When it signs in or renews, it sends your machine's name, its operating system (macOS, Windows or Linux) and izzy's version, which your account page
            lists so you can sign a machine out.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">Check or end the sign-in</Heading>
        <Table Items="Commands" Caption="Account commands" Size="ControlSize.Sm" Class="site-params" Bordered="false">
            <Columns>
                <TemplateColumn Title="Command" Width="Length.Rem(9)"><code>@context!.Name</code></TemplateColumn>
                <TemplateColumn Title="What it does">@context!.Text</TemplateColumn>
            </Columns>
        </Table>
        <TextBlock>
            To sign out a machine you no longer have, use your account page. Code you already created stays yours.
            See the <Anchor Href="docs/cli/license">License</Anchor>.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">Build servers</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            A build server can't approve a code. Make a key on your account page and set it on the server as the
            <Code>IZZY_KEY</Code> environment variable. izzy uses it in place of a sign-in:
        </TextBlock>
        <Code Block Language="yaml">@Workflow</Code>
        <TextBlock>
            The key is shown once, when you make it. Store it as a secret. To stop a key from working, delete it on
            your account page.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">Without an account</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            The components, the IzzyBlazor App starter, <Code>izzy init</Code> and <Code>izzy add crud</Code> need no
            account. When an item needs one, izzy says so and stops with exit code 4.
        </TextBlock>
    </ChildContent>
</ArticlePage>

@code {
    private sealed record Row(string Name, string Text);

    private static readonly Row[] Commands =
    [
        new("izzy account", "Shows whether this machine is signed in, your plan, and when the sign-in renews."),
        new("izzy logout", "Signs this machine out and removes the account's templates from it."),
    ];

    private const string Workflow = """
        - run: izzy add block hero
          env:
            IZZY_KEY: ${{ secrets.IZZY_KEY }}
        """;
}
